 filled with ideas; stocked with knowledge; stored with information.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Reading maketh a full man.", "reference": "1625, Francis [Bacon], “Of Studies”, in The Essayes […], 3rd edition, London: […] Iohn Haviland for Hanna Barret, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Having the attention, thoughts, etc., absorbed in any matter, and the feelings more or less excited by it.", "tags": [], "examples": ["She's full of her latest project."], "quotes": [{"text": "Everyone is now full of the miracles done by cold baths on decayed and weak constitutions.", "reference": "1693, [John Locke], “§7”, in Some Thoughts Concerning Education, London: […] A[wnsham] and J[ohn] Churchill, […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Filled with emotions.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The heart is so full that a drop overfills it.", "reference": "1848, James Russell Lowell, The Vision of Sir Launfal:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Impregnated; made pregnant.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Ilia, the fair, […] full of Mars.", "reference": "1697, Virgil, “(please specify the book number)”, in John Dryden, transl., The Works of Virgil: Containing His Pastorals, Georgics, and Æneis. […], London: […] Jacob Tonson, […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(poker, postnominal) Said of the three cards of the same rank in a full house.", "tags": ["postpositional"], "examples": ["nines full of aces", "I'll beat him with my kings full!"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(chiefly Australia) Drunk, intoxicated.", "tags": ["Australia"], "examples": ["Mr. Coniff: That is the only evidence you gave of his being intoxicated, that his hat was on the side? […] Mr. Coniff: That is the only indication you gave the committee when you were asked if the judge was full, that his hat was on the side of his head; is that right?"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["abounding", "brimful", "bursting", "chock-a-block", "chock-full", "full up", "full to bursting", "full to overflowing", "jam full", "jammed", "jam-packed", "laden", "loaded", "overflowing", "packed", "rammed", "stuffed", "pregnant", "complete", "thorough", "entire", "total", "glutted", "gorged", "sated", "satiate", "satiated", "satisfied", "stuffed", "baggy", "big", "large", "loose", "outsized", "oversized", "voluminous", "drunk"], "antonyms": ["empty", "incomplete", "partial", "empty", "hungry", "starving", "close-fitting", "small", "tight", "tight-fitting"]}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adverb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/fʊl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[fʊɫ]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/fʉl/", "tags": ["Northern Ireland", "Scotland"]}], "forms": [], "senses": [{"definition": "(archaic) Fully; quite; very; thoroughly; completely; exactly; entirely.", "tags": ["archaic", "not comparable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Prospero:\nI have done nothing but in care of thee,\nOf thee, my dear one, thee, my daughter, who\nArt ignorant of what thou art; naught knowing\nOf whence I am, nor that I am more better\nThan Prospero, master of a full poor cell,\nAnd thy no greater father.", "reference": "1610–1611 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tempest”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act I, scene ii]:"}, {"text": "[…] full in the centre of the sacred wood", "reference": "1697, Virgil, “(please specify the book number)”, in John Dryden, transl., The Works of Virgil: Containing His Pastorals, Georgics, and Æneis. […], London: […] Jacob Tonson, […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "You know full well what makes me look so pale.", "reference": "1819, John Keats, Otho the Great, act IV, scene I, verse 112:"}, {"text": "This cupboard[…]\n this other one,\n His true wife's charge, full oft to their abode\n Yielded for daily bread the martyr's stone,", "reference": "1880, Dante Gabriel Rosetti, William Blake, lines 9–12:"}, {"text": "It is full strange to him who hears and feels,\n When wandering there in some deserted street,\n The booming and the jar of ponderous wheels,[…]", "reference": "1874, James Thomson, The City of Dreadful Night, section IX:"}, {"text": "I turned my head, and as I lay gasping in the throes of that awful struggle I could see that Leo was off the rock now, for the lamplight fell full upon him.", "reference": "1886 October – 1887 January, H[enry] Rider Haggard, She: A History of Adventure, London: Longmans, Green, and Co., published 1887, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "Serene, smiling, enigmatic, she faced him with no fear whatever showing in her dark eyes.[…]She put back a truant curl from her forehead where it had sought egress to the world, and looked him full in the face now,[…].", "reference": "1910, Emerson Hough, chapter I, in The Purchase Price: Or The Cause of Compromise, Indianapolis, Ind.: The Bobbs-Merrill Company, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/fʊl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[fʊɫ]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/fʉl/", "tags": ["Northern Ireland", "Scotland"]}], "forms": [{"word": "fulls", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Utmost measure or extent; highest state or degree; the state, position, or moment of fullness; fill.", "tags": [], "examples": ["I was fed to the full."], "quotes": [{"text": "The swan's-down feather,\n That stands upon the swell at full of tide.", "reference": "c. 1606–1607 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Anthonie and Cleopatra”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene ii]:"}, {"text": "Sicilian tortures and the brazen bull,\n Are emblems, rather than express the full\n Of what he feels.", "reference": "1693, Decimus Junius Juvenalis, John Dryden, transl., “[The Satires of Decimus Junius Juvenalis.] The Third Satyr”, in The Satires of Decimus Junius Juvenalis. Translated into English Verse. […] Together with the Satires of Aulus Persius Flaccus. […], London: […] Jacob Tonson […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "[…] he had tasted their food, and found it so palatable that he had eaten his full before he knew it.", "reference": "1911, Berthold Auerbach, Bayard Taylor, The villa on the Rhine:"}, {"text": "Early next morning we were over at the elk carcass, and, as we expected, found that the bear had eaten his full at it during the night.", "reference": "2008, Jay Cassell, The Gigantic Book Of Hunting Stories:"}, {"text": "When he had eaten his full, they set to work again.", "reference": "2010, C. E. Morgan, All the Living: A Novel:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "täydellisyys"}, {"language": {"code": "pl", "name": "Polish"}, "word": "pełnia"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(of the moon) The phase of the moon when its entire face is illuminated, full moon.", "tags": [], "examples": ["It is like, that the brain of man waxeth moister and fuller upon the full of the moon: …", "a. 1656, Joseph Hall, Josiah Pratt (editor), Works, Volume VII: Practical Works, Revised edition, 1808 page 219,\nThis earthly moon, the Church, hath her fulls and wanings, and sometimes her eclipses, while the sha